from a1 import t
C4='The sacraments'
t('real-presence', C4, 'The Real Presence of Christ in the Eucharist', 'The bread and wine are only symbols of Jesus’ body and blood.',
 ["Catholics believe that at Mass, by the power of the Holy Spirit and the words of the priest, the bread and wine become the Body and Blood, Soul and Divinity of Christ (the Church calls this transubstantiation: the appearance of bread and wine remains, while what they truly are changes). Jesus said, “This is my body… this is my blood” (Matthew 26:26-28), not “this represents”. In John 6 he says, “unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink his blood, you have no life in you” (6:53), and when many disciples left because “this is a hard saying”, he did not soften it or call after them to say he was speaking in a figure (6:60-66). The verb he uses for “eat” changes in John 6:54 to a word meaning to chew or gnaw.",
  "Paul warns that whoever eats and drinks unworthily “will be guilty of profaning the body and blood of the Lord” and “eats and drinks judgement on himself” (1 Corinthians 11:27-29), which would make no sense if it were only a symbol. The earliest Christian writers, such as Ignatius of Antioch (about AD 107) and Justin Martyr (about 155), say plainly that the Eucharist is the flesh of Jesus Christ."],
 [('John 6:35-71','The bread of life discourse'),('Matthew 26:26-29','This is my body, this is my blood'),('Luke 22:19-20','Do this in remembrance of me'),('1 Corinthians 10:16-17','A participation in the blood and the body of Christ'),('1 Corinthians 11:23-29','Guilty of the body and blood of the Lord')],
 [1322,1323,1324,1373,1374,1375,1376,1377,1378,1413],['Eucharist','Real Presence','Transubstantiation','John 6'],['mass-sacrifice','confession'])

t('mass-sacrifice', C4, 'Is the Mass a sacrifice?', 'Christ’s sacrifice was once for all, so the Mass cannot repeat it.',
 ["The Church agrees that Christ died once for all (Hebrews 9:26-28; 10:10-14), and the Mass does not repeat or add to that sacrifice. It makes the one sacrifice of Calvary present for us, as the Passover meal made the Exodus present to each generation. At the Last Supper, Jesus took the bread and wine and said, “This is my body, which is given for you… this is my blood… poured out for you”, in the language of sacrifice and covenant. The Church’s worship in Revelation shows a “Lamb standing as though slain” in the heavenly liturgy (Revelation 5:6).",
  "Malachi foretold a “pure offering” in every place from the rising to the setting of the sun (Malachi 1:11), which the earliest Christians (for example the Didache, about AD 100) saw fulfilled in the Eucharist. The priest acts in the person of Christ; the sacrifice is Christ’s own, and we join our lives to his."],
 [('Hebrews 9:11-14','Christ entered once for all with his own blood'),('Malachi 1:11','A pure offering from the rising of the sun to its setting'),('Luke 22:14-20','The Last Supper'),('Revelation 5:6-12','The Lamb as though slain'),('1 Corinthians 10:16-21','The cup of blessing; the table of the Lord')],
 [1362,1363,1364,1365,1366,1367,1368],['Mass','Sacrifice','Eucharist'],['real-presence'])

t('confession', C4, 'Confession to a priest', 'Only God can forgive sins; we do not need to confess to a man.',
 ["Only God forgives sins, and in Jesus God has given this power to men. On Easter evening Jesus breathes on the apostles and says, “Receive the Holy Spirit. If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven; if you retain the sins of any, they are retained” (John 20:22-23). A priest would not be able to “retain” a sin unless he first knew it, which implies that the sins are named. Jesus had already shown that “the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins” (Mark 2:10), and the crowd praised God for giving “such authority to men” (Matthew 9:8). Paul speaks of the “ministry of reconciliation” given to the apostles (2 Corinthians 5:18-20), and James tells the sick to call the elders, and to “confess your sins to one another” (James 5:14-16).",
  "In the sacrament of Penance the priest acts in the person of Christ. The penitent’s sorrow, confession, penance and the priest’s absolution together express and bring about God’s forgiveness. The seal of confession is absolute: a priest may never reveal anything he hears."],
 [('John 20:21-23','Whose sins you forgive are forgiven'),('Matthew 18:18','Whatever you loose on earth is loosed in heaven'),('2 Corinthians 5:18-20','The ministry of reconciliation'),('James 5:14-16','Confess your sins to one another'),('Mark 2:5-12','The Son of Man has authority to forgive sins')],
 [1440,1441,1442,1443,1444,1445,1461,1462,1463,1464,1465,1466,1467],['Confession','Reconciliation','Penance'],['church-authority'])

t('infant-baptism', C4, 'Baptising babies', 'Baptism requires faith, so babies should not be baptised.',
 ["Baptism is God’s gift, not a human achievement. The New Testament speaks of whole households being baptised (Acts 16:15, 33; 1 Corinthians 1:16), and the word “household” in that time included children. Peter said on Pentecost, “the promise is for you and for your children” (Acts 2:38-39). Baptism takes the place of circumcision (Colossians 2:11-12), which was given to boys at eight days old. Jesus said, “Let the children come to me” (Matthew 19:14).",
  "The faith of the Church, parents and godparents supports the child until he or she can profess it personally. The Church baptises infants because original sin affects every person, and because grace is a free gift. Christians have baptised infants from the earliest times: Origen (about 248) said the Church received this tradition from the apostles."],
 [('Acts 2:38-39','The promise is for you and your children'),('Acts 16:31-34','He and all his household were baptised'),('Colossians 2:11-12','Baptism as the new circumcision'),('Matthew 19:13-15','Let the children come to me'),('John 3:5','Born of water and the Spirit')],
 [1250,1251,1252,1253,1254,1255],['Baptism','Infant baptism'],['baptismal-regeneration'])

t('baptismal-regeneration', C4, 'Does baptism save?', 'We are saved by faith alone; baptism is only a symbol.',
 ["The New Testament speaks of baptism in very strong terms. Jesus says, “unless one is born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God” (John 3:5). Peter says, “Repent, and be baptised… for the forgiveness of your sins” (Acts 2:38), and, “baptism… now saves you” (1 Peter 3:21). Ananias tells Paul, “Rise and be baptised and wash away your sins” (Acts 22:16), and Paul says we are saved “by the washing of regeneration and renewal in the Holy Spirit” (Titus 3:5). Baptism is not a symbol only: through it God washes away original sin and all personal sin, gives new life in Christ and makes us members of his Church.",
  "This is not “works salvation”: the baptised do nothing; they receive. The Church teaches that God is not bound by his sacraments, and that those who die for Christ without baptism (baptism of blood) or who seek God sincerely and would have been baptised had they known (baptism of desire) can be saved."],
 [('John 3:3-6','Born of water and the Spirit'),('Acts 2:38','Repent and be baptised for the forgiveness of sins'),('1 Peter 3:20-21','Baptism now saves you'),('Titus 3:5','The washing of regeneration'),('Romans 6:3-4','We were buried with him by baptism')],
 [1213,1214,1215,1216,1257,1258,1259,1260],['Baptism','Salvation','Regeneration'],['infant-baptism','faith-and-works'])

t('celibacy', C4, 'Priestly celibacy', 'Priests should marry. The Bible says a bishop should be the husband of one wife.',
 ["Celibacy for priests in the Latin Church is a discipline, not a doctrine, and it has exceptions: some Eastern Catholic Churches have married priests, and married Protestant clergy who become Catholic may be ordained. Jesus and Paul were both unmarried. Jesus said some “have made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. Let the one who is able to receive this receive it” (Matthew 19:12), and Paul says the unmarried man is “anxious about the things of the Lord” and has an undivided heart (1 Corinthians 7:32-35). “The husband of one wife” (1 Timothy 3:2) forbids a second marriage; it does not command marriage.",
  "The Church asks its priests, who are called to give themselves entirely to God’s people, to live as Christ did, as a sign of the life to come, when “they neither marry nor are given in marriage” (Matthew 22:30)."],
 [('Matthew 19:10-12','For the sake of the kingdom of heaven'),('1 Corinthians 7:7-8, 32-35','Paul on the unmarried life'),('Matthew 22:30','In the resurrection they neither marry nor are given in marriage'),('1 Timothy 3:2','Husband of one wife')],
 [1579,1580,1618,1619,1620],['Celibacy','Priesthood','Virginity'],[])

t('women-priests', C4, 'Why only men as priests?', 'The male priesthood is discrimination.',
 ["The Church teaches that she has no authority to ordain women to the priesthood (John Paul II, Ordinatio Sacerdotalis, 1994). It is not a judgement on women’s dignity or ability. Jesus, who treated women with great respect and had women disciples, chose twelve men as apostles, though it went against some of the customs of his day, and the apostles in turn appointed men as their successors. Mary, the greatest of the saints, was not an apostle. In the early Church, there were many women saints, prophets and teachers (Priscilla, Phoebe, Junia), and women have been Doctors of the Church, but none were ordained priests.",
  "The priest acts “in the person of Christ the Bridegroom” toward the Church, his Bride, which is why the sign of a man is fitting. Women and men are equal in dignity but have different roles, as the Church sees it."],
 [('Mark 3:13-19','Jesus chooses the twelve'),('Luke 6:12-16','He called his disciples and chose twelve'),('Galatians 3:28','Neither male nor female in Christ'),('2 Timothy 2:2','Entrust to faithful men who will teach others')],
 [1577,1578,1579],['Priesthood','Ordination','Women'],['church-authority'])
